sections.s2_technical:
401 Unauthorized is returned when authentication is missing or invalid. The request carried no credentials, or an expired or malformed token. The correct client response is to authenticate and retry. By specification, a 401 response should include a WWW-Authenticate header describing how to authenticate, for example indicating a Bearer token is expected.

```java
// 401: no or invalid token → client must authenticate
if (token == null || !tokenService.isValid(token)) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED)
            .header("WWW-Authenticate", "Bearer")
            .body("Authentication required");
}
```

403 Forbidden is returned when the client is properly authenticated, so the server knows who they are, but their identity lacks the permission or role needed for this action. Retrying with the same token will not help, because the problem is not identity but authorization. For example, Rajesh is a regular customer and tries to access an admin-only report; his token is valid, but his role is CUSTOMER, so he gets 403.

```java
// 403: authenticated but lacks the required role
if (!user.hasRole("ADMIN")) {
    return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN)
            .body("You do not have permission for this resource");
}
```

The order of checks matters. First authenticate, which answers who are you. If that fails, return 401. Only after a successful authentication do you check authorization, which answers are you allowed. If that fails, return 403. You never return 403 to an unauthenticated client, because you cannot judge their permission without knowing who they are.

A security nuance that interviewers love: sometimes you deliberately return 404 instead of 403 to avoid leaking existence. If Rajesh asks for Priya's private account and he is authenticated but not authorized, a strict 403 tells him the account exists but he cannot see it. For sensitive resources, some banks return 404 to hide whether the resource exists at all, trading precise semantics for privacy. This is a deliberate choice, documented, not an accident.

Another nuance is that a 401 is retryable after obtaining fresh credentials, which is why clients often react to 401 by refreshing an expired token and retrying automatically. A 403 is not retryable with the same identity, so the client should not loop; instead it should surface a permission error to the user or request elevated access through a separate flow.

In Spring Security, these map naturally: an AuthenticationEntryPoint handles authentication failures and returns 401, while an AccessDeniedHandler handles authorization failures and returns 403. Method security annotations like PreAuthorize with a role expression trigger a 403 when the authenticated user lacks the role.

A senior nuance: confusing the two is a common and costly mistake. Returning 403 when the token simply expired makes clients give up instead of refreshing, while returning 401 when a user genuinely lacks a role makes clients loop forever trying to re-authenticate, never realising the real problem is permission. Getting the pair right is essential for correct client behaviour and clean security auditing.

